cartridge "PERSUASION/COMPUTE'S GAZETTE/1986"

' PERSUASION
' A ColecoVision game based on "Persuasion", COMPUTE!'s Gazette, October
' 1986 (Commodore 64, BASIC with a small machine-language raster routine;
' the listing names no author). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made
' by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game (orig/persuasion.bas; the ML part, orig/raster.dis, only
' splits the border colour between the two halves): North and South each
' move one token in turn, North first, one diagonal step on a diamond of
' 30 stones (7 rows of 3, 4, 5, 6, 5, 4, 3; a token can never leave the
' stones it can reach diagonally, so both share the same 30). The stone a
' token lands on changes (lines 820, 520-580):
'   stone      North lands       South lands
'   neutral    touched           touched
'   touched    half North        half South
'   half North North (+1 North)  touched (undone)
'   half South touched (undone)  South (+1 South)
'   North      neutral (-1 N)    no change
'   South      no change         neutral (-1 S)
' First to 16 wins (THE NORTH / SOUTH IS THE WINNER); 15 all is a tie.
' Both tokens may share a stone.
'
' Kept from the listing: the board, the starting stones (row 0 and row 6,
' middle), the moves (diagonals only, lines 490-510), the colour table,
' the scoring, North first, 16 to win and the 15-15 tie, the end words.
'
' Liberties:
' - 1 PLAYER mode (new): you are North against a computer South that
'   sees only the board (it weighs the stone it lands on, twice, plus the
'   best other stone less 3 a step from there; ties go round the four
'   directions). The original was for two players only.
' - A game lasts at most 300 moves (150 each; MOVES LEFT on screen):
'   then the higher score wins, equal scores tie. Without it two careful
'   sides can undo each other's half stones for ever (model.mjs games
'   never ended); first to 16 and 15-15 still end it at once. One move
'   repeated back and forth cannot win (a stone gained is lost on the
'   next visit); the bot checks it loses to the computer.
' - Both controllers work for whoever is to move (the original passed one
'   joystick). A move needs a fresh diagonal push: holding the stick does
'   not repeat, so the other side's move never fires a held stick.
' - Colours: neutral stays red, touched green; the sides are blue and
'   yellow (the listing's blue and grey) on bevelled stones; a half stone
'   is light over dark, as in the listing. The token to move is white,
'   the other grey (the listing swapped yellow and black).
' - The token slides to its new stone; sounds and the title tune
'   ("Gentle Persuasion") are new; the title shows the rules.
' - At the end: the message, then the button (or 30 seconds) returns to
'   the title, no PLAY AGAIN key.
'
' Controls: either controller; the stick only (diagonal pushes) in play;
' up/down and a side button on the title.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128;
' frame loop protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI and the
' display off. Music first in the sound table, the title tune once.

